Random experiment = multiple possible outcomes, cannot predict exact outcome.
Ex. coin toss, dice roll

Trial = execution of random experiment
Ex. Trial 1 → outcome: head; Trial 2 → outcome: tail; tossed 100 times → 100 trials

Outcome = result of a trial
Ex. toss → outcome = head

Sample space = all possible outcomes
Ex. Coin → sample space = {H, T};   Dice → sample space = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}

Event = collection of outcome that satisfy our condition
Ex. Even numbers of dice = {2, 4, 6} ← compound event;   
      numbers greater than 3 = {4, 5, 6) ← compound event; 
      getting 5 = {5} ← simple event
Subset of sample space  ( E⊆S )


Types of events:
Simple events = only one outcome
Compound event = more than 1 outcomes
Independent events = two events are not dependent on each other; ex. Dice and coin are rolled together, events of dice and coin are not dependent of each other
Dependent events = opposite of independent events
Mutually exclusive events = events that cannot happen at the same time; ex. Event 1 = dice outcome is odd, Event 2 = dice outcome is even
	Both event cannot come true at a same time
Certain events = where probability = 1; ex. Result after rolling dice is greater than 0
Impossible event = where probability = 0; ex. Result of rolling of dice is 0


— — 

Probability = Likeliness of something to happen


Types of Probabilities:
Theoretical probability = calculated purely from maths; ex. 100 coins are tossed, probability for heads to appear = ½, probability for tails to appear = ½
Empirical probability = probability based on real life experiment only; 100 real coins tossed in real life in real environment, head appeared for 57 times, tails appeared for 43 times

As number of trials increases empirical probability approaches theoretical probability


— —

Random variable = mathematical representation of outcome of trial; ex. Head = 0, Tails = 1, because PC only understands numbers


Types of random variables:
Discrete random variables: you can list all of them. Finite in number.
Ex. number of students, no. of books, no. of visible colours to human eye
Continuous random variables: you cannot list them all. Infinite in number.
Ex. height, weight, numbers between 1 and 2

